Solve the system of linear equations below:
\begin{align*}
14x - 9y &= 17 \\
-19x - 21y &= 3
\end{align*}
To solve this system, we can use the method of elimination. First, we want to eliminate one of the variables by multiplying the equations by appropriate constants. Let's multiply the first equation by 21 and the second equation by 9 to make the $y$ coefficients equal:

First equation (multiplied by 21):
\begin{align*}
294x - 189y &= 357
\end{align*}

Second equation (multiplied by 9):
\begin{align*}
-171x - 189y &= 27
\end{align*}

Now, subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate $y$:
\begin{align*}
(294x - 189y) - (-171x - 189y) &= 357 - 27 \\
294x + 171x &= 330 \\
465x &= 330
\end{align*}

Divide both sides by 465 to solve for $x$:
\begin{align*}
x &= \frac{330}{465} \\
x &= \frac{22}{31}
\end{align*}

Now, substitute $x$ back into one of the original equations to find $y$. We'll use the first equation:
\begin{align*}
14\left(\frac{22}{31}\right) - 9y &= 17 \\
\frac{308}{31} - 9y &= 17
\end{align*}

Multiply both sides by 31 to eliminate the fraction:
\begin{align*}
308 - 279y &= 527 \\
-279y &= 527 - 308 \\
-279y &= 219
\end{align*}

Divide both sides by -279 to solve for $y$:
\begin{align*}
y &= \frac{219}{279} \\
y &= \frac{73}{93}
\end{align*}

